 europe and we all know  the pandemic is not over  and there's still a lot of work for all  of us  ahead of us ladies and gentlemen  lisbon the city we are in today  is a living example of renewal  and recovery the devastating  earthquake in this very spot  265 years ago  was a human tragedy it was a disaster  and it came at a time of great change  and progress  for europe a time when traditions and  institutions were being challenged  intellectual freedom was blossoming  rivalries between nations were on the  rise  the parallels to today's latest disaster  may not be perfect but history  does have a habit of rhyming  i say this because even before the  pandemic hit  europe was all go already going through  a period of profound  and rapid change from digitalization  to decarbonization to global  fragmentation  and the need for us to get back on our  feet today  and build a better way of doing things  is just  as important as it was in the 18th  century  the point is we can learn from  lisbon how to build back better  how to remodel our societies to harness  change and progress  and you see it with your own eyes when  you admire  prasa do commercial a living monument of  renewal  of course times are different and we  must do things  differently in today's world  and in this spirit i very much welcome  the preparation  and cooperation of the portuguese  recovery and resilience plan  your priorities and ambitions  mirror exactly those of next generation  eu  the european union's large recovery plan  for  reforms and investment  and i want to thank you antonio prime  minister kosta  for your support in brokering this  historic agreement last july  next generation eu allows the commission  to raise 750 billion euros  on the market today in order to invest  it  in a stronger future for tomorrow that's  our game  that's our goal this is an urgent and  unprecedented necessity  but it is for an urgent and  unprecedented situation  we need to repair our social fabric  and on 